Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Jean DERMINE
Author-Workplace-Name: INSEAD
Title: Intermédiaires financiers et contrôles publics
Abstract: Le présent essai apporte quelques éléments de réflexion de nature économique sur l’intervention des pouvoirs publics dans le secteur financier, tant au niveau du contrôle des institutions privées que de l’intervention directe dans le marché. En référence plus particulière au cas belge, les principales questions abordées concernent la signification des données comptables, le niveau des fonds propres et la fragilité du système financier. L’analyse débouche sur des propositions concrètes concernant en particulier l’utilisation de valeurs d’acquisition et un mode de calcul de l’exigence en fonds propres et une réduction des frais de fonctionnement sont suggérés pour assainir le système financier et enfin, la création d’un organisme officiel d’assurance des dépôts est proposée pour réduire le risque d’une crise financière en Belgique.
Length: 24
Series: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Sciences Economiques
Number: 1982012
Creation-Date: 1982-03-01
File-URL: http://www.jstor.org/stable/40723669
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvre:1982012

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Philippe MICHEL
Author-Workplace-Name: Université de Paris, Centre de Mathématiques Economiques
Title: Expected Equilibrium and Fix-Price Equilibrium in a Simple Macroeconomic Model : Equivalence Theorems and Stability
Abstract: The recent development of macroeconomic models with fixed prices and adjustment of quantities has shown the importance of considering the type of unemployment in the economy, since this determines the effects of economic policy decisions. The fundamental concept of this theory, namely fix-price equilibrium with quantity rationing, presents similarities with Walrasian equilibrium. Its interpretation is based on tatonnements on quantities instead of tatonnements on prices.
Another approach based on firms’ expectations is studied here in a simple macroeconomic model. This approach considers “monopolistic behavior on quantities” by firms; surprisingly, this asymmetrical treatment of firms and households is very close to fix-price equilibrium where agents are treated symmetrically. Equivalence theorems allow replacing the tatonnement assumption with the one of locally perfect foresight by firms. Moreover, there is an equivalence between the optimization behavior of firms and the stability of fix-price equilibrium with respect to adjustment of quantities.

Length: 20
Series: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Sciences Economiques
Number: 1982013
Creation-Date: 1982-03-01
File-URL: http://www.jstor.org/stable/40723670
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvre:1982013

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: John E. BRENNAN
Author-Workplace-Name: DRI Europe
Title: Limited Dependent Variable Models Applied to the Workings of Belgian Price Regulation
Abstract: In recent times many western governments have either considered or actually applied some form of price regulation in order to combat an accelerating worldwide inflation that has respected no one’s national boundaries. The price controls that were applied by governments pressured to take action have been typically collections of pragmatic administrative procedures, requiring the creation of their own operating bureaucracies. Often neither the original arguments that were raised in favor nor the resulting measures enacted were seen by economists as grounded in any coherent piece of orthodox economic theory. There has been many theoretical articles written about these aspects of price regulation, however, empirical studies attempting to discover the consequences of these systems at the level of the individual decision-makers involved have been few due to the unavailability of relevant data. This recent Belgian experience then offers a unique opportunity as a large volume of material has been made available for econometric study.
Length: 24
Series: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Sciences Economiques
Number: 1982014
Creation-Date: 1982-03-01
