Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvre:1998011

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Catherine BAUMONT
Author-Workplace-Name: LATEC (CNRS), University of Burgundy
Author-Name: Jean-Marie HURIOT
Author-Workplace-Name: LATEC (CNRS), University of Burgundy
Title: The monocentric model and after
Abstract: Von Thünen’s monocentric model is considered as one of the foundations of spatial economics. Most of its assumptions have been transfered from agricultural to urban space by New Urban Economics. This transposition gave new impetus both to the monocentric model and urban economics. Yet the urban monocentric model, because of its strong economic and spatial assumptions, fails to explain the formation of cities. The economics agglomeration, and more generally New Economic Geography, propose to solve this problem by considering endogenous formation of spatial concentrations, i. e. by changing radically the approach of space. We attempt to identify the lines of continuity and the main changes from von Thünen to the contemporary urban microeconomics, and to understand in what way continuity is an obstacle to innovation, and how change has made it possible to renew urban economics
Classification-JEL: B00, R00
Length: 21
Series: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Sciences Economiques
Number: 1998012
Creation-Date: 1998-03-01
File-URL: http://sites.uclouvain.be/econ/DP/REL/1998012.pdf
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvre:1998012

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Robert B. EKELUND JR. 
Author-Workplace-Name: Auburn University, USA
Author-Name: Robert F. HÉBERT
Author-Workplace-Name: Auburn University, USA
Title: Railroads, engineers, and the developement of spatial economics in France
Abstract: This articles traces a little-known French tradition in spatial economics that was advanced by state engineers who were trained at the Ecole des Ponts et Chaussées and worked for the State Corps engineers. Spurred in part by changes in power technology, especially the advent of the railroad, these engineers pioneered the spatial aspects of competition in a macroeconomic framework. The contribution of Jules Dupuit (1804 - 1866) and Emile Cheysson (1836 - 1910) are especially singled out for analysis, and are shown to have anticipated later important developments in spatial economic theory.
Classification-JEL: B10,R10, N73
Length: 18
Series: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Sciences Economiques
